assertion  XSH.fabs.001
anchor     functions/fabs.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fabs.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       fabs() of -3.5, 3.5, -0.25, 0.25, -100.0625, MAX and -MAX (MAX the
           largest finite value of the type) returns 3.5, 3.5, 0.25, 0.25,
           100.0625, MAX and MAX.
test       t/001-values.c

assertion  XSH.fabs.002
anchor     functions/fabs.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fabs.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       fabs() of a NaN and of its negation returns a NaN.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/002-nan.c

assertion  XSH.fabs.003
anchor     functions/fabs.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fabs.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       fabs(+0) and fabs(-0) return +0 (a zero with the sign bit clear).
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/003-zero.c

assertion  XSH.fabs.004
anchor     functions/fabs.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fabs.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       fabs(+Inf) and fabs(-Inf) return +Inf.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/004-infinity.c

assertion  XSH.fabs.005
anchor     functions/fabs.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fabs.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       With each rounding direction that can be selected (at least two are
           needed), fabs(-0.1) is 0.1 and fabs(0.1) is 0.1, as written in the
           type of the call.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/005-rounding-mode.c

assertion  XSH.fabs.006
anchor     functions/fabs.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fabs.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.23
class      required
since      1988
says       With errno holding EDOM before the call, fabs(-3.5) leaves it
           unchanged and, when exception flags are in use, raises none of the
           invalid, divide-by-zero and overflow exceptions.
test       t/006-no-error.c
